WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.460
A trust boundary is any point where data crosses from a less trusted context into code that will make decisions.

2
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:18.120
In Rust web applications, the obvious boundaries are HTTP request bodies, JSON payloads, query strings, path parameters, headers, and cookies.

3
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:29.900
Frameworks can extract those values into useful structures, but extraction does not prove that the values are allowed, complete, current, or meaningful for the operation being requested.

4
00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:33.140
Command-line tools and services have their own boundaries.

5
00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:41.160
Arguments, environment variables, configuration files, and file contents often arrive as strings or bytes that must be interpreted.

6
00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:49.740
A deployment variable may be missing, a config value may use an old format, or a command-line path may point somewhere the program should not access.

7
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:56.080
Rust's standard library gives you careful APIs, but the application still decides which values are acceptable.

8
00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.080
Distributed systems add more boundary cases.

9
00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:06.060
Message queues can deliver stale messages, duplicated messages, or messages produced by an older version of a service.

10
00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:11.360
Socket protocols can contain partial frames or unexpected sequence data.

11
00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:18.740
Third-party API responses can change shape, omit fields, or return values outside the assumptions captured by local structs.

12
00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:22.600
Deserialized data should still be checked before it drives business behavior.

13
00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:29.520
FFI boundaries deserve special attention because they connect Rust code with code that may not share Rust's safety guarantees.

14
00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:39.380
Data passed into or out of foreign functions should be constrained, length-checked, and converted into safe Rust representations as soon as practical.

15
00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:49.160
The same mindset applies across all boundaries: identify where data enters, validate it once with purpose, and move only accepted values into the trusted part of the program.
